.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt DUMP_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dump_minixfs
.Nd dump a MINIX file system in the format of BSD dump
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 0123456789u
.Op Fl D Ar dumpdates
.Op Fl L Ar label
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Fl T Ar date
.Fl f Ar file
.Ar image
.Op Ar path
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes the MINIX file system in
.Ar image
to
.Ar file
in NetBSD's UFS1 dump format.
The dump can be read by
.Xr restore_minixfs 8
and the
.Xr restore 8
commands of NetBSD, FreeBSD and Linux.
All MINIX versions, both byte orders and Minix-vmd are supported;
see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
The source image is not modified.
.Pp
The dump is in the byte order of the image.
Inode
.Em n
of the image is inode
.Em n
+ 1 in the dump, since
.Xr restore 8
takes 2 for the root.
Directories go in as those of 4.4BSD, whatever the entries of the image,
and the last record of each file even when it lies in a hole, as the
last block of a file on UFS always exists.
.Pp
A dump of level 0 holds the whole file system.
One of level
.Em n
holds what changed since the last dump of a lower level, as the
dumpdates file tells: the files whose mtime or ctime is not before its
date, the directories that hold them, and the directories that have
directories in them, as BSD dump does.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 0 No .. Fl 9
The level; 0 by default.
.It Fl u
When the dump is done, note it in the dumpdates file under the name of
.Ar image
as it was given, in place of the note of the same level.
.It Fl D Ar dumpdates
The dumpdates file, with a line for each image and level: the name, the
level and the date as
.Xr ctime 3
prints it, as BSD dump writes it.
A blank, tab, newline or backslash of the name is written as a
backslash and three octal digits, as the name is the first word.
The default is
.Pa /etc/dumpdates .
.It Fl L Ar label
The label of the dump, 16 characters at most;
.Dq none
by default.
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Read an image that holds the file system in the tracks of one side of a
disk only, as
.Xr minixfs 1
does.
.It Fl T Ar date
Dump what changed since
.Ar date ,
given as
.Xr ctime 3
prints it, instead of since the date in the dumpdates file.
.It Fl f Ar file
Where the dump goes;
.Ql -
is standard output, which must not be a terminal.
.El
.Pp
With
.Ar path ,
only the directory
.Ar path
and what is below it go in, with the directories above it, at level 0
and without
.Fl u ,
as
.Xr dump 8
does for a subdirectory.
.Pp
A file system that is not marked clean is dumped with a warning.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if anything failed, and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A full dump, noted, and a later one of what changed since:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# dump_minixfs -0u -f /backup/usr.0 usr.img
# dump_minixfs -1u -f /backup/usr.1 usr.img
.Ed
.Pp
Move a file system onto a new one of another version, hard links and
all:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 3 -s 65536 new.img
$ dump_minixfs -f - old.img | restore_minixfs -r -f - new.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr dump 8 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr restore 8 ,
.Xr restore_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh CAVEATS
V1 keeps no ctime, so a change of mode, owner or name alone does not put
a file in a dump of a higher level.
.Pp
A dump goes on one file; it is never split over volumes.
